Eczema (Atopic Dermatitis)
Dermatology & Soft Tissue
Intense pruritus, Flexural erythematous plaques, Xerosis

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: DST-016
Reason for Visit: Acute exacerbation of severe, generalized pruritus causing complete insomnia, secondary excoriation, and severe emotional distress.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient, Partner
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported a patient with a known history of atopy experiencing an uncontrollable, full-body flare of intense pruritus and weeping skin lesions. The patient had not slept for 48 hours and was highly distressed. Urgent out-of-hours assessment was required to exclude secondary bacterial superinfection, break the severe itch-scratch cycle with potent pharmacological intervention, and provide immediate psychological stabilization.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "I am literally tearing my skin off. I haven't slept in two days, and every joint on my body is raw, weeping, and burning."
History of Present Illness: The patient reports a lifelong personal history of atopy (asthma and allergic rhinitis), with baseline xerosis. Over the past 72 hours, triggered by the onset of dry winter months and a recent upper respiratory tract infection, they have developed an intense, uncontrollable pruritus. The rash consists of erythematous ill-defined scaly plaques, heavily concentrated in a flexural distribution involving the antecubital and popliteal fossae. Continuous scratching has led to acute excoriation, focal lichenification, and the formation of vesicles with serous exudate in the most affected flexural creases. The intense pruritus has resulted in profound sleep deprivation and acute psychosocial distress.
Functional Status: Independent, but functionally impaired by severe pruritus and fatigue.
Social/Safety Concerns: Severe acute psychological distress and sleep deprivation; high risk of secondary bacterial colonization of excoriated areas.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 138/86 | HR: 98 | Temp: 37.1 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 70 Kg
General: Visibly exhausted, agitated, continuously rubbing and scratching at their arms and behind their knees.
Physical Exam: The skin demonstrates generalized profound xerosis. There are extensive, erythematous, ill-defined scaly plaques predominantly affecting the antecubital and popliteal fossae, neck, and wrists. These flexural erythematous plaques exhibit severe acute excoriations, scattered micro-vesicles, and serous weeping. Chronic lichenification is evident on the wrists. Despite the weeping, there is no golden crusting (impetiginization), spreading cellulitic erythema, fluctuance, or tender regional lymphadenopathy to suggest overt staphylococcal superinfection.
Environmental Observations: Central heating is running high, contributing to the dry environment.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. OTC hydrocortisone 1% has been ineffective. No regular emollient use.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Severe Atopic Dermatitis (Eczema) Flare - Status: Acute Exacerbation
Differentials:

Eczema Herpeticum - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of monomorphic, punched-out erosions or grouped vesicles, and lack of systemic fever or severe malaise.

Scabies - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Sparing of the finger webs, absence of visible burrows, and classic flexural rather than extensor/truncal predominance.

Contact Dermatitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The generalized, symmetrical flexural distribution and known atopic history, which strongly points to an endogenous flare rather than a specific external allergen.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate (due to acute distress and sleep deprivation)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient is experiencing a severe, acute flare of atopic dermatitis characterized by unbearable pruritus, serous weeping, and severe sleep deprivation. Triage correctly identified the combination of widespread broken skin and acute psychological distress as requiring urgent medical intervention. Immediate after-hours assessment was clinically mandated to rule out eczema herpeticum or severe impetiginization, neither of which were present. Crucially, the visit allowed for the immediate prescribing of a short course of oral corticosteroids and potent topical steroids to rapidly arrest the severe inflammatory cascade, break the agonizing itch-scratch cycle, and prevent an imminent presentation to the Emergency Department for an acute crisis of intractable pain and itch.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Prescribed oral Prednisolone 25mg daily for 5 days to rapidly control systemic inflammation. Prescribed topical Betamethasone dipropionate 0.05% ointment BD for the severely inflamed flexures. Advised immediate commencement of a thick, unfragranced emollient applied liberally QID. Prescribed oral Promethazine 25mg at night for sedation and itch control.
Positive Behavioral Change: By taking a short, lukewarm shower instead of a hot one, and gently patting your skin dry, you will prevent stripping away your natural skin oils and immediately reduce the stinging. By applying a thick layer of moisturizer within 3 minutes of getting out of the shower, you will lock that water into your skin, creating a physical barrier that drastically stops the itch.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Not required.
DME/Safety (Optional): Not required.
Follow-up: Regular GP review in 5 days to monitor the tapering of oral steroids and transition to long-term maintenance therapy.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

If the weeping areas develop a thick, yellow, honey-colored crust, if the skin becomes hot and swollen, or if you develop painful, punched-out little sores and a fever, present immediately to the nearest Emergency Department.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Eczema flares can be unpredictable; if the skin breaks down further, bacterial infections can develop rapidly.
Clinical Course: The oral steroids and sedating antihistamine should provide significant relief and allow you to sleep tonight. The redness and weeping will dry up over the next 3-5 days.
SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Monitor closely for any spreading, hot redness or thick yellow crusts, which signal a bacterial infection requiring antibiotics.
PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Your immune system is severely overreacting to the dry air and recent cold, causing massive inflammation. The oral steroids will act like a fire extinguisher to put out the systemic inflammation, while the creams repair the damaged skin barrier.
Actionable Steps: Apply the steroid ointment only to the actively red/itchy areas. Apply the plain moisturizer everywhere else, at least 4 times a day.

